# Shatter — Collision

This file defines every impact in the game and what it does. The bodies it names
are defined in `specs/ship.md`, `specs/weapons.md`, `specs/rocks.md`,
`specs/saucer.md`, and `specs/field.md`.

## Every body is a circle

Whatever a body is drawn as, it collides as a circle of the radius below, centred
on its position.

| Body | Radius |
| --- | --- |
| The ship | `SHIP_R` (`14`) |
| A bullet | `BULLET_R` (`3`) |
| A Large rock | `ROCK_RADIUS.large` (`46`) |
| A Medium rock | `ROCK_RADIUS.medium` (`26`) |
| A Small rock | `ROCK_RADIUS.small` (`14`) |
| The saucer | `SAUCER_R` (`18`) |
| A saucer bullet | `SAUCER_BULLET_R` (`3`) |
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
| A torpedo | `TORPEDO_R` (`6`) |
{{/if}}
| The star's core | `CORE_R` (`30`) |

Two bodies touch when the shortest wrapped separation between their centres, as
`specs/field.md` defines it, is at most the sum of their radii. Bodies touching
across a wrap seam therefore collide.

Collision is swept or continuous. Two bodies whose paths over a tick bring them
within the sum of their radii at any point of that tick collide on it, however
fast either was travelling and however far either moved. No body passes through
another in a tick.

## What each pair does

| Pair | Resolution |
| --- | --- |
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}| A bullet and a rock | The bullet is removed and the rock's health falls by `1`, as `specs/rocks.md` states. The hit that takes health to `0` destroys the rock, which splits and scores. |
{{else}}| A bullet and a rock | The bullet is removed and the rock is destroyed, which splits and scores. |
{{/if}}
| A bullet and the saucer | Both are removed, and the saucer scores. |
| A bullet and the core | The bullet is absorbed and removed. Nothing scores. |
| A rock and the core | The rock is recycled, as `specs/rocks.md` states. Nothing scores. |
| The ship and a rock | The ship is destroyed and a life is lost. |
| The ship and the saucer | The ship is destroyed and a life is lost. |
| The ship and a saucer bullet | The ship is destroyed and a life is lost, and the bullet is removed. |
| The ship and the core | The slide, below. No life is lost and the ship is not destroyed. |
| A rock and a rock | Nothing. They pass through each other, both keeping their velocities. |
| The saucer and a rock | Nothing. They pass through each other. |
| A saucer bullet and a rock | Nothing. The bullet passes over the rock, which is unharmed. |
| A saucer bullet and the core | The bullet is absorbed and removed. |
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}
| A torpedo and a rock | The rock is destroyed outright whatever its size or remaining health, and the torpedo is removed. The rock splits and scores exactly as a gun kill of the same rock would. A torpedo destroys only the rock it strikes. |
| A torpedo and the saucer | Both are removed, and the saucer scores. |
| A torpedo and the core | The torpedo is absorbed and removed. Nothing scores. |
| A torpedo and the ship | Nothing. The torpedo passes through the ship, which is unharmed, and stays in flight. |
| A torpedo and a saucer bullet | Nothing. |
{{/if}}

The ship's destruction is subject to the respawn grace `specs/progression.md`
states: inside that window the three lethal pairs above cost nothing and the ship
passes through unharmed.

## The slide along the core

The core is solid but never lethal to the ship. On contact:

1. The ship's centre is pushed back out along the direction from the star's centre
   to the ship, to a distance of `CORE_R + SHIP_R` (`44`) from `(STAR_X, STAR_Y)`.
2. The component of the ship's velocity heading into the core is removed, and the
   component along the surface is kept unchanged.
3. The ship's facing is unchanged, and the player keeps full control throughout.

The ship therefore grazes around the core's surface and slides free.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}

## The two fragment fans

A destroyed rock leaves two fragments at its position (`specs/rocks.md`). Each
fragment's velocity is the destroyed rock's velocity on the tick it was destroyed,
plus a kick that depends on what destroyed it.

| Killed by | The kick each fragment takes |
| --- | --- |
| A bullet | `SPLIT_KICK` (`90`) perpendicular to the bullet's travel at the moment it landed, the two fragments kicked to opposite sides |
| A torpedo | `TORPEDO_SCATTER` (`240`) directed radially outward from the destroyed rock's centre, the two fragments blasted to opposite sides |

The perpendicular of a gun kill is taken from the bullet's own direction of
travel, not from the rock's course, so the fan lies across the shot whatever the
rock was doing. Both fragments then obey the well like any rock.
{{else}}

## The fragment fan

A destroyed rock leaves two fragments at its position (`specs/rocks.md`). Each
fragment's velocity is the destroyed rock's velocity on the tick it was destroyed,
plus a kick of `SPLIT_KICK` (`90`) perpendicular to the bullet's travel at the
moment it landed, the two fragments kicked to opposite sides.

The perpendicular is taken from the bullet's own direction of travel, not from the
rock's course, so the fan lies across the shot whatever the rock was doing. Both
fragments then obey the well like any rock.
{{/if}}
